<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      The window ground for the frames Android draws before Compose has one.

      `pi_window_background` is the **day** value here and the night value lives in
      `values-night/`: at this point in the launch there is no theme information at
      all — pi's `theme` setting is a file the engine has not been asked to read —
      so the only polarity available is the system's, which is exactly the input
      `PiThemeLoader.load` itself falls back to. Day = pi's `light` export page
      (`#EFEEEE`), night = pi's `dark` export page (`#21252C`); both are the same
      literals as `PiPalette.Light.pageBg` / `PiPalette.Dark.pageBg`, so this
      resource can never disagree with the Compose canvas it hands over to.
      `MainActivity` replaces it with the *resolved* theme's `pageBg` as soon as
      that exists, which is what covers a pi theme pinned to the opposite polarity.

      docs/pi-android-ui-spec.md §2.1 maps export.pageBg -> Surface and
      export.cardBg -> SurfaceContainer; only the first is needed before Compose.

      The launcher icon's ground is the artwork's own dark, `#11141D`, sampled from
      the PNG the user picked (`ui-redesign/reference/新图标-原始.png`). Its other two
      colours live literally in `drawable/ic_launcher_foreground.xml` — `#F3F3F3` for
      the pi wordmark and `#5BD470` for the >_ prompt — so there is no
      `ic_launcher_foreground` colour resource any more. The icon still does not follow
      the pi theme: an icon that changed with a theme file would be an icon nobody
      could recognise.
    -->
    <color name="pi_window_background">#EFEEEE</color>
    <color name="ic_launcher_background">#11141D</color>
</resources>
